.TH "decode" 3 "Version 0.9.0" "mbedded-cbor" \" -*- nroff -*-
.ad l
.nh
.SH NAME
decode \- a decoded tree of CBOR and its encode
.SH SYNOPSIS
.br
.PP
.SS "Functions"

.in +1c
.ti -1c
.RI "std::expected< std::reference_wrapper< item const >, \fBerror\fP > \fBcbor::encoded_cbor_data_item\fP (\fBowning\fP< item > const &tree, item const &tagged)"
.br
.RI "Gives the root of the item that a tag 24 (RFC 8949 3\&.4\&.5\&.1) holds in its byte string\&. "
.ti -1c
.RI "std::expected< \fBowning\fP< item >, \fBerror\fP > \fBcbor::decode\fP (std::string_view const encoded)"
.br
.RI "Decodes the one top-level item in \fRencoded\fP into a tree of cbor::item\&. "
.ti -1c
.RI "std::expected< \fBref\fP< std::span< iovec const > >, \fBerror\fP > \fBcbor::encode_iov\fP (item const &root)"
.br
.RI "Writes the tree at \fRroot\fP into a list of \fRstruct iovec\fP pieces, not into one buffer\&. "
.ti -1c
.RI "std::expected< \fBref\fP< std::span< iovec const > >, \fBerror\fP > \fBcbor::encode_iov\fP (\fBowning\fP< item > const &root)"
.br
.RI "Writes the tree that \fRroot\fP owns into a list of \fRstruct iovec\fP pieces, and refers to its long strings\&. "
.ti -1c
.RI "template<class Target>"
.br
requires detail::encoding::is_target_v<std::remove_reference_t<Target>>
.br
std::expected< std::size_t, \fBerror\fP > \fBcbor::encode\fP (Target &&out, item const &root)
.br
.RI "Writes the tree at \fRroot\fP and gives the count of bytes written\&. "
.in -1c
.SH "DESCRIPTION"
.PP 
\fR\fBcbor::decode\fP\fP reads one top-level item of a \fRstd::string_view\fP into a tree of \fRcbor::item\fP\&. \fR\fBcbor::encode\fP\fP and \fR\fBcbor::encode_iov\fP\fP write a tree as CBOR\&. Include \fR<\fBcbor/cbor\&.hpp\fP>\fP\&.

.PP
The tree copies each text and byte string into one memory zone that the result owns\&. It does not view the input, and it stays valid after the input ends\&. \fR\fBcbor::decode\fP\fP reads no byte after the item, so a CBOR sequence (RFC 8742) is read item by item\&. It reads every head of the item, so every limit counts each head\&. A count in a head reserves nothing: an array that claims more elements than bytes are left gives \fRtoo_little_data\fP before it reads an element\&.
.SH "THE TREE"
.PP
\fRtype()\fP gives the kind of an item, a \fRcbor::type\fP\&. \fRadditional_information\fP gives the low 5 bits of its initial byte (RFC 8949, section 3)\&. \fRget<T>()\fP gives the value as \fRstd::expected<T, error>\fP, as \fR\fBcbor::lazy::get\fP<T>()\fP reads it\&. A node of another kind gives \fRincorrect_type\fP, and a number that \fRT\fP does not hold gives \fRnumber_out_of_range\fP\&.

.PP
.IP "\(bu" 2
An integer type: an integer; a negative integer gives its value, -1 minus the argument\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRdouble\fP: a float, or an integer that a \fRdouble\fP holds exactly\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRbool\fP, \fRstd::nullptr_t\fP: \fRfalse\fP or \fRtrue\fP, \fRnull\fP\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRcbor::simple_value\fP: \fRfalse\fP, \fRtrue\fP, \fRnull\fP, \fRundefined\fP or another simple value\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRstd::string_view\fP, \fRstd::span<std::byte const>\fP: a text, a byte string\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRcbor::typed_array\fP: a typed array (RFC 8746), its tag number and its bytes\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRstd::span<cbor::item const>\fP: an array, its elements\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRstd::ranges::chunk_view<std::span<cbor::item const>>\fP: a map, each key and its value as one chunk of two items\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRstd::pair<std::uint64_t, cbor::item const *>\fP: a tag, a bignum or a \fRshared_reference\fP, the tag number or the index, and the content or the item that the tag 28 of that index marks\&.
.PP

.PP
Every \fRT\fP except the pair passes a tag that it does not interpret, as \fR\fBcbor::lazy::get\fP\fP does\&. \fRvisit(f)\fP calls \fRf\fP once with the value of the node, as \fRstd::visit\fP does: a negative integer as \fRcbor::int128\fP, a map as the \fRchunk_view\fP, a tag or a bignum as the pair, a typed array as \fRcbor::typed_array\fP and a \fRshared_reference\fP as the \fRcbor::item const *\fP of its target\&. \fRvisit\fP exists where \fRcbor::int128\fP exists\&.

.PP
A bignum (tag 2 or 3) whose magnitude fits in 64 bits is the same value as a basic integer (RFC 8949, section 3\&.4\&.3)\&. \fR\fBcbor::decode\fP\fP makes it an \fRunsigned_integer\fP or a \fRnegative_integer\fP item\&. A larger bignum stays an \fRunsigned_bignum\fP or a \fRnegative_bignum\fP\&. Its content is a byte string item that holds a copy of the magnitude\&.
.SH "TAGS 24, 28, 29 AND 55799"
.PP
A tag 24 is a tag item over its byte string\&. \fRcbor::encoded_cbor_data_item(tree, item)\fP gives the root of the embedded item, decoded as a top-level item of its own\&. For a tag 24 whose content is no byte string it gives \fRincorrect_type\fP\&.

.PP
Tag 28 marks a value as shared\&. Tag 29 with the index n stands for the value of the n-th tag 28\&. \fR\fBcbor::decode\fP\fP resolves both tags, with a table for one call\&. A tag 28 makes no node and marks the node of its content\&. A tag 29 is a \fRshared_reference\fP item\&. That item can be the item itself or an item above it, so the tree can hold a cycle\&. An index with no tag 28 before it gives \fRsharedref_index_not_marked\fP, and \fR28(29(0))\fP gives \fRsharedref_not_defined\fP\&.

.PP
A lazy, \fRclone\fP, \fRat_path\fP, \fR\fBcbor::path::query\fP\fP and \fR\fBcbor::diagnostic_notation\fP\fP read tag 28 and tag 29 as tags over their content\&. They resolve nothing, check no index and give no error of their own\&. A step and \fRget\fP pass both tags, as they pass every tag that they do not interpret\&. \fR\fBcbor::structs\fP<T>::decode\fP and \fRcbor::schema<T>\fP read no value under them and give \fRinvalid_tag\fP\&.

.PP
Tag 55799 (RFC 8949, section 3\&.4\&.6) marks bytes as CBOR\&. \fR\fBcbor::decode\fP\fP reads one leading tag 55799 as its content, so the root of its tree is the content\&. A second tag 55799, and a tag 55799 inside an item, stay tags\&.
.SH "ENCODE"
.PP
The target of \fR\fBcbor::encode\fP\fP is one of these:

.PP
.IP "\(bu" 2
a container of bytes, for example \fRstd::string\fP or \fRstd::vector<std::byte>\fP: \fRencode\fP appends to it;
.IP "\(bu" 2
a fixed span of bytes: \fRencode\fP writes into it, allocates nothing, and gives \fRno_buffer_space\fP when an item does not fit;
.IP "\(bu" 2
a writer of the program with \fRallocate\fP, \fRappend\fP and \fRdone\fP\&. An error of the writer comes back as a \fR\fBcbor::error\fP\fP\&.
.PP

.PP
\fR\fBcbor::structs\fP\fP writes no tag 28 and no tag 29\&.

.PP
\fRcbor::encode(out, item)\fP writes a tree with the additional information that each item keeps\&. Each integer, length and tag number keeps the width of its head on the wire\&. A float keeps the width of its type, and a NaN keeps its bits\&. A bignum that fits in 64 bits is written in its shortest head\&. A \fRshared_reference\fP gives \fRunsupported_value\fP\&. \fRencode_cycles(out, tree)\fP writes tags 28 and 29 only for a cycle\&. It walks the tree before it writes, depth first, and follows each \fRshared_reference\fP to its item\&. An item that the walk reaches again while it is inside that item gets a tag 28\&. Each such edge is a tag 29 with the index of that mark\&. A value that two references reach without a cycle is written in full each time\&. The path through references can be deeper than the tree, so \fRencode\fP can give \fRnesting_depth_exceeded\fP for a tree that \fR\fBcbor::decode\fP\fP gave\&. A tree of \fRclone\fP keeps tags 28 and 29 as tags, and \fRencode\fP writes them as they are\&.

.PP
\fRcbor::encode_iov(item)\fP writes a tree into a list of pieces as \fRstruct iovec\fP, with their storage, which lives as long as the result\&. The pieces are for \fRwritev\fP, \fRsendmsg\fP, \fRIORING_OP_WRITEV\fP and \fRIORING_OP_SENDMSG\fP\&. The result is \fRstd::expected<\fBcbor::ref\fP<std::span<iovec const>>, \fBcbor::error\fP>\fP\&. The pieces are for sending only\&. Hold the result until the call returns, or until the last completion of the request comes\&. \fRencode_iov\fP writes into blocks of 16 KiB and copies each byte once\&.

.PP
\fRcbor::encode_iov(*tree)\fP, with the \fR\fBcbor::owning\fP<cbor::item>\fP that \fR\fBcbor::decode\fP\fP gives, does not copy a text or a byte string longer than \fRCBOR_ENCODER_COPYBREAK\fP bytes (4096 by default): its piece points into the tree, and the result holds the tree alive\&. \fRcbor::encode_iov(**tree)\fP copies every string\&.
.SH "ERRORS"
.PP
Each function gives \fRstd::expected\fP with a \fR\fBcbor::error\fP\fP\&. \fR\fBcbor::encoded_cbor_data_item\fP\fP throws \fRstd::logic_error\fP for an item that is not a tag 24 of that tree\&.

.PP
 
.SH "EXAMPLES"
 
.PP
.nf
    std::string const bytes = "\\x82\\x18\\x01\\x61\\x61"s;  // [1, "a"], the 1 with a one\-byte argument
    auto const tree = cbor::decode(bytes);
    auto const second = (*tree)\->get<std::span<cbor::item const>>()\->at(1)\&.get<std::string_view>();  // "a"
    std::string out;
    auto const size = cbor::encode(out, **tree);  // 5; out == bytes
.fi
.PP
 
